00:00:53.172 --> 00:00:56.635
Right, the magic of this
show is to try three foods

00:00:56.635 --> 00:00:58.154
at drastically different price points

00:00:58.154 --> 00:00:59.707
to determine which is the most--

00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:00.567
- Worth it!

00:01:00.567 --> 00:01:03.014
- This week, we are tasting
Britain's favorite food.

00:01:03.014 --> 00:01:04.465
- Curry.
- Curry!

00:01:04.465 --> 00:01:05.666
- Fish and chips.

00:01:05.666 --> 00:01:06.870
- It's curry.

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.369
We've established it.
- We've already said it, man.

00:01:08.369 --> 00:01:09.202
- It's definitely curry.

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:10.798
Right guys, our first stop is

00:01:10.798 --> 00:01:12.997
Whitecross Street Market in East London.

00:01:12.997 --> 00:01:14.258
- I've been a cockney.

00:01:14.258 --> 00:01:15.630
- Whitecross Street.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.244
- Hey Rich, what's
Whitecross Street Market?

00:01:17.244 --> 00:01:19.131
- It is the oldest market in London!

00:01:19.131 --> 00:01:20.346
(pained exclaiming)
- Nice, awesome!

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:21.480
(firm smacking)
(pained exclaiming)

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:22.763
(amused chuckling)

00:01:22.763 --> 00:01:24.023
- You excited?

00:01:24.023 --> 00:01:25.804
- Yeah, I feel sore now.

00:01:25.804 --> 00:01:28.837
(bongo drum rolling)

00:01:28.837 --> 00:01:31.285
- [Jaz] My name is Jaz, I'm
here at Whitecross Street.

00:01:31.285 --> 00:01:33.599
This is my store, and it's called Karuna.

00:01:33.599 --> 00:01:35.117
- [Rich] Where did the
name Karuna come from?

00:01:35.117 --> 00:01:36.877
- My mother helped me a lot initially

00:01:36.877 --> 00:01:40.181
to start off the business,
and her name's Aruna.

00:01:40.181 --> 00:01:42.692
I've been at Whitecross
Street for around 17 years.

00:01:42.692 --> 00:01:45.291
I'm one of the longest
standing traders here now.

00:01:45.291 --> 00:01:47.369
So, we specialize in Indian street food.

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:48.462
We basically do three different

00:01:48.462 --> 00:01:50.207
vegetarian curries every day.

00:01:50.207 --> 00:01:51.852
- The word masala, is that sauce?

00:01:51.852 --> 00:01:53.172
- Yeah basically, that's
exactly what it is.

00:01:53.172 --> 00:01:56.604
You've got tomato puree, cumin,
got the coriander powder,

00:01:56.604 --> 00:01:59.056
turmeric, chili powder, garam masala.

00:01:59.056 --> 00:02:00.760
So that's the basic mix.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.643
You can formulate that
into each individual curry.

00:02:03.643 --> 00:02:07.296
Today, you'll be having the
Gobi Matar Cauliflower and Pea,

00:02:07.296 --> 00:02:10.925
a Split Lentil Tarka Dal,
and the Bombay Potato

00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:12.758
on a bed of rice at four pounds.

00:02:12.758 --> 00:02:15.186
(percussive music)

00:02:15.186 --> 00:02:16.634
- It looks damn fine.

00:02:16.634 --> 00:02:17.584
It looks really good.

00:02:17.584 --> 00:02:18.864
- [Rich] Look how colorful it is.

00:02:18.864 --> 00:02:20.428
- [Charles] Oh, god, it's like a rainbow.

00:02:20.428 --> 00:02:21.925
- Do you remember the ice creams you'd get

00:02:21.925 --> 00:02:23.848
as a kid that was the three colors?

00:02:23.848 --> 00:02:25.614
- This is like the curry
version Neapolitan.

00:02:25.614 --> 00:02:27.399
- Three in one with some sides.

00:02:27.399 --> 00:02:28.232
- Cheers?

00:02:28.232 --> 00:02:29.065
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

00:02:29.065 --> 00:02:30.604
Let's go, I'm gonna sweat a bit.

00:02:30.604 --> 00:02:31.437
- What?

00:02:31.437 --> 00:02:32.854
- Mmm, oh my god.

00:02:33.944 --> 00:02:35.390
That's really good.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:36.849
- Well, you're makin' a complete mess.

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:38.042
- Yeah I know, I'm sorry.

00:02:38.042 --> 00:02:38.927
- [Rich] Do you want a napkin?

00:02:38.927 --> 00:02:39.872
- [Charles] No.

00:02:39.872 --> 00:02:41.652
Yeah, maybe.

00:02:41.652 --> 00:02:44.305
- It was perfect on the
spice factor for me.

00:02:44.305 --> 00:02:46.325
I don't like too much
spice, makes my nose run.

00:02:46.325 --> 00:02:48.849
- You got the cauliflower
there, you got the rice as well.

00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:51.676
That kinda adds what you would
be missing in a meat dish.

00:02:51.676 --> 00:02:53.891
The lentil gets a 10 out of 10 for me.

00:02:53.891 --> 00:02:54.977
- Oh, heck yeah.

00:02:54.977 --> 00:02:56.799
Really impactful flavor, I would say.

00:02:56.799 --> 00:02:57.745
- Umph.
- Yeah!

00:02:57.745 --> 00:02:58.849
- It's an umph-y curry, isn't it?

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:00.082
- No, definitely.

00:03:00.082 --> 00:03:01.829
It's like each part of the
meal is a different part

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:04.534
of town, and you're sort of
going through one to the other.

00:03:04.534 --> 00:03:06.711
- That's why this is such a good dish.

00:03:06.711 --> 00:03:08.016
You can't get bored of either one.

00:03:08.016 --> 00:03:09.884
- No, you can barely get
the lid on when we got it,

00:03:09.884 --> 00:03:11.775
it's filled to bursting.

00:03:11.775 --> 00:03:13.935
For four pounds, I don't know
what more we could ask for.

00:03:13.935 --> 00:03:16.435
How you doin' over there, Joe?

00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:20.863
(upbeat jazz music)

00:03:20.863 --> 00:03:23.165
(cheerful music)

00:03:23.165 --> 00:03:23.998
- Curry fact.

00:03:23.998 --> 00:03:25.758
- Curry fact.

00:03:25.758 --> 00:03:28.792
London contains more Indian restaurants

00:03:28.792 --> 00:03:30.937
than Mumbai and Delhi combined.

00:03:30.937 --> 00:03:32.295
- Really, is that true?

00:03:32.295 --> 00:03:34.537
Where did you get that one from?

00:03:34.537 --> 00:03:36.412
Well then, it's obviously not true.

00:03:36.412 --> 00:03:39.048
And now, we are off to Brick Lane.

00:03:39.048 --> 00:03:39.881
- Yes sir.

00:03:39.881 --> 00:03:42.459
Brick lane has some of the
most curry houses in London.

00:03:42.459 --> 00:03:45.647
It's really one of London's
cultural hot spots, and--

00:03:45.647 --> 00:03:46.674
- Where curry was born.

00:03:46.674 --> 00:03:48.354
- Not quite where curry was born.

00:03:48.354 --> 00:03:51.789
- You do know where curry
originated, don't you?

00:03:51.789 --> 00:03:52.661
- Korea?

00:03:52.661 --> 00:03:54.354
(tickled chuckling)

00:03:54.354 --> 00:03:56.937
(upbeat music)

00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:06.766
(big band jazz music)

00:04:09.195 --> 00:04:11.274
- My name is Ronny, we're Nazrul.

00:04:11.274 --> 00:04:13.138
This is the oldest
restaurant in Brick Lane.

00:04:13.138 --> 00:04:15.272
It's all about atmosphere,
it's all about food.

00:04:15.272 --> 00:04:17.645
It's about quality,
the traditional dishes.

00:04:17.645 --> 00:04:20.656
These are like, madras,
bhuna, how they cook in India,

00:04:20.656 --> 00:04:23.005
how they cook in Bangladesh,
how they cook in Kashmir.

00:04:23.005 --> 00:04:25.888
But you know, chef
specialties from chef secrets.

00:04:25.888 --> 00:04:27.850
He can't even tell me,
if I got at the kitchen

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.413
and ask him, he's not gonna tell me.

00:04:30.413 --> 00:04:31.634
That's the secret.

00:04:31.634 --> 00:04:33.782
We get all the ingredients fresh here,

00:04:33.782 --> 00:04:36.046
nothing is precooked,
everything is grilled,

00:04:36.046 --> 00:04:38.414
marinated first, to a tandoori oven.

00:04:38.414 --> 00:04:42.618
He makes his own sauce, and he
prepared it from his version.

00:04:42.618 --> 00:04:44.895
It's sweet and sour, little bit of kick,

00:04:44.895 --> 00:04:46.798
but it's not spicy, it's
not gonna burn your tongue.

00:04:46.798 --> 00:04:50.570
You'll be havin' Chicken
Tikka Blast at 15.95.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:52.835
And let me know, let my food talk, not me.

00:04:52.835 --> 00:04:55.791
- I like the little tomato
crown there, it's cool.

00:04:55.791 --> 00:04:58.144
- [Rich] And the multicolored
rice is just awesome.

00:04:58.144 --> 00:05:00.379
- Yeah, it's just a well
presented meal, I like it.

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:02.173
- [Rich] Oh, I got a
big thing of something.

00:05:02.173 --> 00:05:03.156
A bit of bark.

00:05:03.156 --> 00:05:04.303
- I feel that's a cinnamon stick?

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:05.821
- Sorry, cinnamon.

00:05:05.821 --> 00:05:06.899
Everyone should tear their own bread.

00:05:06.899 --> 00:05:09.490
- A man should tear his own bread, yeah.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:10.990
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

00:05:12.896 --> 00:05:14.244
- Oh, it's really nice.

00:05:14.244 --> 00:05:15.077
- Yeah, this is good.

00:05:15.077 --> 00:05:16.390
- It's not hot, it's just a nice

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:17.777
little flicker of spice, right?

00:05:17.777 --> 00:05:18.610
- Yeah.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:19.443
What I really like about it, is

00:05:19.443 --> 00:05:21.259
it's actually quite sweet as well.

00:05:21.259 --> 00:05:22.350
- I think I could eat a lot of this.

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.341
- Cheers, buddy.

00:05:23.341 --> 00:05:24.246
- Gotta have beer with Indian food.

00:05:24.246 --> 00:05:25.996
- Oh yeah, of course.

00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:28.568
I feel like the more I eat this,

00:05:28.568 --> 00:05:30.817
the more I discover about what's in it.

00:05:30.817 --> 00:05:32.067
- It's actually quite complex.

00:05:32.067 --> 00:05:34.447
There's been a lot of love
and care gone into the

00:05:34.447 --> 00:05:35.787
